{"command": "cd /workspace && python3 - <<'PY'
doc = '''//! Progress bar ("feedback") implementation for xcp.
//!
//! The progress display shows the amount transferred, the percentage
//! complete, the transfer rate and an estimate of the time remaining.  It is
//! deliberately simple: two lines of plain text, redrawn in place, with no
//! dependency on any terminal library.
//!
//! Ownership of the display state lives with a background thread.  Copy
//! workers report their progress through a channel; the thread applies the
//! updates to its own state and renders the display on a cadence which is
//! independent of the copy loop.  Keeping the terminal work out of the copy
//! workers matters when many of them are active, since contending for the
//! display lock would otherwise serialise the copies.
//!
//! Reporting is done through a [`Reporter`] handle which can be cloned freely
//! and shared between the walk worker and the copy workers.  The walk worker
//! reports the total byte count as files are discovered, so that the bar
//! reflects the whole transfer even before it starts, while the copy workers
//! report the number of bytes actually written.
//!
//! Reporting detail
//! ----------------
//!
//! Progress is reported in bytes.  The total is the sum of the sizes of all
//! the regular files discovered so far, which means that for a directory copy
//! the bar can grow as the walk proceeds.  The current value is the number of
//! bytes the copy workers have reported as written.
//!
//! The rate is a cumulative average over the transfer so far rather than an
//! instantaneous measurement.  That keeps the displayed figure smooth at the
//! cost of lagging behind sudden changes in throughput.  The remaining time
//! is derived by dividing the outstanding byte count by that rate.
//!
//! Both figures are computed with integer arithmetic because the display only
//! has a resolution of one second.  This matches the original implementation,
//! including its habit of dividing by zero when no progress has been made at
//! all, which aborts the copy with an error rather than silently leaving the
//! display in place.
//!
//! Thread interaction
//! ------------------
//!
//! Reports are sent over a channel and the copy workers wait for each report
//! to be picked up before continuing.  This keeps the displayed state
//! consistent with the work actually performed, and means a failure of the
//! display thread is noticed by the copy loop at once, aborting the copy
//! rather than losing the display silently.
//!
//! The channel is closed when the driver finishes; the feedback thread then
//! exits and the join handle is reaped by the caller.
//!
//! Terminal handling
//! -----------------
//!
//! The terminal size is queried with an ioctl on standard error.  A failure,
//! or a reported width of zero, means the size is unknown and the single line
//! rendering mode is used instead.
//!
//! When the width is known the bar is exactly as wide as the terminal, so
//! writing it leaves the cursor at the end of the line, ready for the line
